  "question": {
    "text": "If $f:[0, \\pi / 2) \\rightarrow R$ is defined as\r\n$f(\\theta)=\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}1 & \\tan \\theta & 1 \\\\ -\\tan \\theta & 1 & \\tan \\theta \\\\ -1 & -\\tan \\theta & 1\\end{array}\\right|$ Then, the range of $f$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$(2, \\infty)$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$(-\\infty,-2]$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$[2, \\infty)$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$(-\\infty, 2]$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have, $f(\\theta)=\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}1 & \\tan \\theta & 1 \\\\ -\\tan \\theta & 1 & \\tan \\theta \\\\ -1 & -\\tan \\theta & 1\\end{array}\\right|$\r\n$=1\\left(1+\\tan ^{2} \\theta\\right)-\\tan \\theta$\r\n$(-\\tan \\theta+\\tan \\theta)+1\\left(\\tan ^{2} \\theta+1\\right)$\r\n$=2\\left(1+\\tan ^{2} \\theta\\right)-0=2 \\sec ^{2} \\theta$\r\n$\\therefore$ Range of $f=(2, \\infty)$"
  }
}